<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamagata Aritomo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1838">1838</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamagata Aritomo (1838–1922) was a Japanese soldier and statesman from the Chōshū domain, regarded as the father of the modern Japanese army. He introduced conscription in 1873, commanded in the First Sino-Japanese War and twice served as prime minister, in 1889–91 and 1898–1900. As one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genro">genro</a>, the elder statesmen, he dominated Japanese politics until his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamagata-aritomo.jpg" alt="Yamagata Aritomo" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamagata_Aritomo_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yamagata was born in Hagi, the castle town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Chōshū Domain</a>, to a low-ranking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> family of the chūgen class. His mother died when he was five and his grandmother Katsu raised him. In 1858 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> as an intelligence agent for the domain, on the recommendation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin">Yoshida Shōin</a>, and then studied under Yoshida at the Shōka Sonjuku. Wounded in the bombardment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimonoseki">Shimonoseki</a> in 1864, he became convinced of the superiority of Western military technology. He served in the Kiheitai, a militia of men from all social classes, and led Chōshū forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> of 1868.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1869 to 1870 Yamagata toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> with Saigō Tsugumichi to study military systems, and he was impressed by Prussian universal conscription. He was the primary driver behind the Conscription Act, which made military service compulsory for all male citizens regardless of class. Peasants resented it as a blood tax and some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> opposed it. As field commander he crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-rebellion">Satsuma Rebellion</a> of 1877, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a>. In 1878 he set up an independent Imperial Army General Staff on the Prussian model, reporting directly to the emperor, which separated military command from civilian government.</p><p>Yamagata became Home Minister in 1883 and centralised local government and the police. As prime minister from December 1889 he oversaw the opening of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> in 1890, and in a speech that March he described a line of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and a line of interest, which he identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a>, in which he commanded the First Army until illness cut short his command, he pressed to double the army from seven to thirteen divisions. His second cabinet began in November 1898; in 1899 civil service ordinances made it harder for party members to hold high bureaucratic posts, and in May 1900 only active-duty generals and admirals could serve as army and navy ministers. He resigned in October 1900.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After 1900 Yamagata was a genrō and rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a>, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a> he was the final authority on military strategy as Chief of the Army General Staff. In 1920-1921 he tried to block the marriage of Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hirohito">Hirohito</a>, citing hereditary colour blindness in the bride's family; he failed, to the harm of his prestige. He died on 1 February 1922 at his villa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawara">Odawara</a> and was given a state funeral. His legacy is contested: in the West and postwar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> he is often depicted as the arch-villain of Japanese militarism, while others see a cautious and pragmatic statesman concerned with national security.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yamagata Aritomo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The genro, an informal council of elder statesmen such as Ito Hirobumi and <b>Yamagata Aritomo</b>, provided coherent direction through the Meiji and Taisho periods, but as this generation died the system produced no institutional successor.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Kido Takayoshi and Takasugi Shinsaku, pupils of Yoshida Shōin, led the loyalists of Chōshū, whose Kiheitai militia, under <b>Yamagata Aritomo</b>, helped seize the domain government; Itō Hirobumi, Inoue Kaoru and Ōmura Masujirō joined them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Officers of the police brigade, many of them of samurai origin from Satsuma itself, proposed to the army commander <b>Yamagata Aritomo</b> that the best swordsmen of the police be sent in; Yamagata, the champion of conscription, agreed reluctantly and named the unit himself.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battotai">Battōtai</a></li><li><blockquote>In Kagoshima, the heavily outnumbered samurai of Saigō Takamori made a last stand against Imperial Japanese Army troops under <b>Yamagata Aritomo</b> and were annihilated.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its men, Kido Takayoshi, Ito Hirobumi and <b>Yamagata Aritomo</b>, led Meiji Japan; it became Yamaguchi Prefecture in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/state/choshu-domain">Choshu Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hagi, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Koki-an. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.41,131.4&amp;year=1892" aria-label="Hagi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7891 917 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yamagata Aritomo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8341 1187h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8341 1187h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.41,131.4&amp;year=1892">What was here in 1892? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
